1
00:00:00,113 --> 00:00:04,567
Welcome! This video shows how to install StocksReport and create your first stock reports.

2
00:00:04,617 --> 00:00:09,068
Pause any time and follow along. All commands are also written in docs/HOW_TO_RUN.md.

3
00:00:09,052 --> 00:00:14,006
Chapter 1 — What you need: Python 3.10 or newer, Git and an internet connection.

4
00:00:14,056 --> 00:00:18,027
Install Python and Git from their official websites if you don’t have them yet.

5
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:23,046
Next, open a terminal: PowerShell on Windows, Terminal on Mac or Linux.

6
00:00:23,096 --> 00:00:25,691
Check that Python is installed: type  python --version  and press Enter.

7
00:00:25,741 --> 00:00:30,196
Then check Git:  git --version   (On Mac/Linux, use python3 if python is not found.)

8
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:25,548
Chapter 2 — One-time setup. You only need to do this once per computer.

9
00:00:25,598 --> 00:00:29,957
Step 1: download the project with git clone.

10
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:34,757
Move into the project folder and switch to the latest version of the code.

11
00:00:34,807 --> 00:00:37,516
Step 2: create a private Python environment for this project.

12
00:00:37,566 --> 00:00:41,504
Step 3: switch the environment on. Mac/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate — Windows: .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

13
00:00:41,554 --> 00:00:45,008
Your prompt now starts with (.venv). Repeat this activate step every time you open a new terminal.

14
00:00:45,058 --> 00:00:50,932
Step 4: install the required packages. This takes 1 to 3 minutes.

15
00:00:50,982 --> 00:00:56,504
Step 5: tell the SEC (a free data source) your name and email — use your own details.

16
00:00:56,554 --> 00:00:59,006
Setup is done! 🎉

17
00:00:55,313 --> 00:01:00,280
Chapter 3 — Generate the reports. Type the stock tickers you want, then the formats.

18
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:02,937
Example: Apple (AAPL), Microsoft (MSFT) and JPMorgan (JPM), in all three formats.

19
00:01:02,987 --> 00:01:22,148
It takes about 20 seconds per stock. Each line shows the verdict, score, price, fair value and data checks.

20
00:01:22,198 --> 00:01:25,880
All files are saved in the  reports  folder.

21
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:30,384
Open the folder with:  start reports  (Windows),  open reports  (Mac) or  xdg-open reports  (Linux).

22
00:01:25,444 --> 00:01:31,155
Chapter 4 — The HTML report. Double-click the .html file to open it in your browser.

23
00:01:31,205 --> 00:01:35,655
At the top: the verdict (Buy / Hold / Sell), the score out of 100 and the confidence.

24
00:01:35,705 --> 00:01:40,160
Key metrics: valuation, margins, growth and risk — each tagged with where the number came from.

25
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:45,666
Benchmark: performance against the S&P 500 (SPY) and the sector ETF, with a 1-year chart.

26
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:50,170
Score breakdown: six areas, each with the numbers behind it.

27
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:54,673
Pros and cons — every point shows the exact figure that triggered it.

28
00:01:54,723 --> 00:01:59,177
The retail investor view and the portfolio manager view.

29
00:01:59,227 --> 00:02:03,682
Fair value and a suggested entry price with a 10% margin of safety.

30
00:02:03,732 --> 00:02:09,188
Data verification: each number checked against SEC filings and Nasdaq prices.

31
00:02:13,669 --> 00:02:17,642
Chapter 5 — The Excel workbook. One file covers every stock you asked for.

32
00:02:17,692 --> 00:02:23,156
Comparison sheet: one row per stock — price, score, verdict, valuation, margins and more.

33
00:02:23,206 --> 00:02:28,184
Summary sheet per stock: verdict, score, pillar scores, fair value and investor views.

34
00:02:28,234 --> 00:02:32,698
Metrics sheet: every metric with its source (computed, Yahoo or SEC).

35
00:02:32,748 --> 00:02:37,221
Benchmark sheet: returns vs the S&P 500 and sector ETF, plus the peer comparison.

36
00:02:37,271 --> 00:02:41,747
Pros-Cons sheet: pros, cons, risks, catalysts, analyst actions and news headlines.

37
00:02:41,797 --> 00:02:46,750
Verification sheet: every data check, colour-coded PASS / WARN / FAIL.

38
00:02:46,818 --> 00:02:50,786
Chapter 6 — The PowerPoint deck: 10 slides per stock, ready to present.

39
00:02:50,836 --> 00:02:53,992
Title slide with the verdict and score.

40
00:02:54,042 --> 00:02:57,196
Key metrics.

41
00:02:57,246 --> 00:03:00,398
Benchmark chart against the S&P 500 and the sector ETF.

42
00:03:00,448 --> 00:03:03,601
Score breakdown.

43
00:03:03,651 --> 00:03:06,805
Pros and cons, with the evidence for each point.

44
00:03:06,855 --> 00:03:10,010
Investor views.

45
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:13,214
Verdict, fair value and entry price.

46
00:03:13,264 --> 00:03:16,415
Data verification summary.

47
00:03:15,692 --> 00:03:24,329
Chapter 7 — The web app. Run  streamlit run app.py  and your browser opens automatically.

48
00:03:24,379 --> 00:03:26,049
Type one or more tickers in the sidebar, separated by commas.

49
00:03:26,099 --> 00:03:33,743
Keep “Verify data” ticked, then click Analyze.

50
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:37,612
Overview: verdict, score, price and the company summary.

51
00:03:37,662 --> 00:03:41,455
Metrics & Benchmark: key numbers, returns and peers.

52
00:03:41,505 --> 00:03:45,303
Pros / Cons with their evidence.

53
00:03:45,353 --> 00:03:49,152
Investor Views: retail investor and portfolio manager perspectives.

54
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:53,021
Verdict & Fair Value, with the suggested entry price.

55
00:03:53,071 --> 00:03:56,823
Data Verification: every check against SEC and Nasdaq.

56
00:03:56,873 --> 00:04:00,326
Download buttons give you the HTML, Excel and PowerPoint files directly.

57
00:04:00,376 --> 00:04:09,077
Enter several tickers to compare them side by side.

58
00:04:09,127 --> 00:04:15,083
The comparison table shows each stock’s score, verdict, valuation, returns and data checks.

59
00:04:21,168 --> 00:04:27,524
Chapter 8 — Check data accuracy against the original sources.

60
00:04:27,574 --> 00:04:34,309
Numbers are compared with SEC filings, Nasdaq prices and accounting checks. Only non-PASS items are listed.

61
00:04:34,359 --> 00:04:44,813
PASS = matches the source. WARN = small definition difference. FAIL = real difference — the report then uses the SEC value.

62
00:04:44,863 --> 00:04:48,817
SKIP means the source does not publish that number, e.g. for non-U.S. companies.

63
00:04:54,747 --> 00:05:01,198
Chapter 9 — Quick fixes for the most common problems.

64
00:05:01,248 --> 00:05:06,216
More help is in docs/HOW_TO_RUN.md, section 9 — Troubleshooting.

65
00:05:06,266 --> 00:05:12,217
That’s it — you can now create reports for any stock. Reports are educational, not financial advice.
